an intensely powerful surge of electromagnetic energy.

737
00:54:51,201 --> 00:54:56,200
The concept originated from nuclear testing conducted by the Navy in the early 1960s.

738
00:54:56,201 --> 00:55:05,200
They found out that a nuclear weapon releases not only the normal things you think of, of light and heat,

739
00:55:09,200 --> 00:55:14,200
but it also releases electromagnetic pulse, EMP.

740
00:55:14,201 --> 00:55:19,900
An EMP, that electromagnetic pulse, will fry circuits.

741
00:55:21,220 --> 00:55:28,980
EMP was less of a threat in the 1960s because most electronics were still based on relatively sturdy vacuum tube technology.

742
00:55:30,240 --> 00:55:40,440
But when we went to things like transistors and silicon chips, that EMP would literally eat those things up.

743
00:55:40,441 --> 00:55:50,120
When a missile carrying an EMP munition nears its target, the munition generates an electromagnetic field and then explodes.

744
00:55:50,720 --> 00:55:56,880
The energy of the explosion then transfers to the electromagnetic field, increasing its size and power.

745
00:55:56,881 --> 00:56:00,940
A lot of military equipment has chips.

746
00:56:01,560 --> 00:56:09,500
And because it has these chips in it, you get a big pulse of electromagnetic energy into them.

747
00:56:09,501 --> 00:56:16,280
It will overload the circuits, they will shut down, and that piece of equipment is in fact dead.

748
00:56:19,380 --> 00:56:23,420
Civilian law enforcement also has a need for a form of mission kill technology.

749
